[38;5;231m⏺[39m Third question — the stub. [38;5;153mlogin()[39m currently returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m synchronously and never touches
  [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m. Since the userId is coming from the login response, something has to produce one.

  [1mWhat differs:[0m whether [38;5;153mlogin()[39m becomes asynchronous. That's the most contagious decision left — every present and
  future caller has to [38;5;153mawait[39m it, and switching a sync function to async later means revisiting each one.

  - [1mKeep the stub, synthesize a userId[0m — the stub returns a fake id (e.g. derived from the username or a UUID). Buys:
    the whole session layer gets built and tested now, with no backend. Costs: the id isn't real, and you'll do the
    async conversion later anyway.
  - [1mMake it a real [38;5;153mfetch[39m, [38;5;153mlogin()[39m becomes async[0m — POSTs to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m, reads [38;5;153muserId[39m off the response. Buys: the
    interface is right from the start; callers are written [38;5;153mawait[39m from day one. Costs: [38;5;153mhttps://api.example.com/login[39m
    doesn't exist, so nothing works end-to-end until a backend does; needs error/network handling now.
  - [1mAsync signature, stubbed body[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is [38;5;153masync[39m and returns a synthesized userId without a network call, with the
    [38;5;153mfetch[39m dropped in later. Buys: the contagious part of the decision is paid once, now, while there's exactly one
    caller; swapping the body later touches only [38;5;153mlogin()[39m. Costs: slightly odd-looking async function with no await in it
    today.

  [1mMy recommendation: the third.[0m The expensive, repo-wide part of this choice is the async signature, not the fetch;
  making it async now while there's a single caller costs one line, and doing it after you've added the other forms
  costs all of them.
